## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Classification

Your code is a **timeout enforcement** pattern with a **ThreadAbortException-as-control-flow** catch. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is obsolete (`SYSLIB0006`).

### Key Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` → `PlatformNotSupportedException` | Fatal at runtime in .NET 8 |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` → `SYSLIB0006` obsolete | Won't compile without suppression, does nothing |
| Cooperative cancellation isn't immediate | Work must check the token — long-running code between checks won't stop instantly |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks timers and callbacks |
| `default(T)` returned on timeout path | Original code had this bug too — `result` was assigned but never returned on abort |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Executes work with a timeout using cooperative cancellation.
    /// </summary>
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        Task<T> task = Task.Run(() => work(), token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException when
            // the token fires, even if work() is still running.
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Prefer this async overload in new code.
    /// </summary>
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        try
        {
            return await work(token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### What Changed and Why

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(...)` | Task-based is idiomatic; uses ThreadPool |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` | `Task.WaitAsync(token)` | Cancellable wait without blocking a thread |
| `thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource(timeout)` | Cooperative cancellation replaces preemptive abort |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | New exception type for cancellation |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed | No equivalent needed — cancellation doesn't auto-rethrow |

### Critical Caveat

**Cooperative cancellation can't force-stop uncooperative code.** If `work()` is a tight CPU loop or a blocking native call that never checks the token, the task will keep running in the background after timeout. Options:

1. **Best:** Modify `work` to accept `CancellationToken` and check it periodically
2. **Escape hatch:** Move uncooperative work to a child `Process` and call `Process.Kill()` on timeout

```csharp
// If you can modify the work to cooperate:
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        ProcessItem(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

The async overload is preferred for new code — it passes the token directly into the work delegate, enabling true end-to-end cooperative cancellation.
